  "verbalization": "Calenduloside C is shown to have a target relationship with VMRC-LCP, which is in turn targeted by multiple compounds, including (2S,3R,4R,5R,6S)-2-[(2R,3R,4S,5S,6R)-5-[(2S,3R,4R)-3,4-dihydroxy-4-(hydroxymethyl)oxolan-2-yl]oxy-4-hydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)-2-[[(1S,2S,4S,6R,7S,8R,9S,12S,13R,16S)-6-methoxy-7,9,13-trimethyl-6-[(3R)-3-methyl-4-[(2R,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxybutyl]-5-oxapentacyclo[10.8.0.02,9.04,8.013,18]icos-18-en-16-yl]oxy]oxan-3-yl]oxy-6-methyloxane-3,4,5-triol and (4S,5S)-5-[(1R,2R)-2-Hydroxy-2-Methyl-5-Oxocyclopent-3-En-1-Yl]-3-Methylidene-4-(3-Oxobutyl)Oxolan-2-One. These compounds are all targeted by VMRC-LCP, which in turn is targeted by Calenduloside C. The evidence suggests that Calenduloside C may act as a modulator of VMRC-LCP, which is a key target in the pathway leading to SMMC-7721. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Calenduloside C to influence the biological activity of SMMC-7721 through its interaction with VMRC-LCP, which is a central node in the target network. The evidence supports a mechanism where Calenduloside C may exert its effects by interacting with VMRC-LCP, thereby influencing the downstream targets that ultimately lead to the effects observed in SMMC-7721.",
